{"url":"https://openlibrary.org/api/books?bibkeys=OLID:OL797308M,OLID:OL7593006M,OLID:OL15343426M,OLID:OL27081017M,OLID:OL36290051M,OLID:OL44097440M&jscmd=details&format=json","file":"data/metadata1000_261025/raw/library-c6eb836bee26bdb1.json","retrieved":"2026-10-25T14:37:09.072Z","status":200,"data":{"OLID:OL797308M":{"bib_key":"OLID:OL797308M","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL797308M/The_physics_of_Star_Trek","preview":"restricted","preview_url":"https://archive.org/details/physicsofstartr000krau","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/298207-S.jpg","details":{"publishers":["Basic Books"],"identifiers":{"librarything":["31361"],"goodreads":["1412215"]},"ia_box_id":["IA150701"],"dewey_decimal_class":["791.45/72"],"covers":[298207],"local_id":["urn:phillips:31867000947130"],"ia_loaded_id":["physicsofstartr000krau"],"lc_classifications":["QB500 .K65 1995"],"key":"/books/OL797308M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL31136A","name":"Lawrence Maxwell Krauss"}],"ocaid":"physicsofstartr000krau","publish_places":["New York"],"subjects":["Star Trek (Television program)","Space sciences","Video games"],"uri_descriptions":["Book review (H-Net)"],"pagination":"xvi, 188 p. :","source_records":["marc:marc_records_scriblio_net/part25.dat:15169401:800","marc:marc_ithaca_college/ic_marc.mrc:173844481:832","ia:physicsofstartr000krau","marc:marc_openlibraries_phillipsacademy/PANO_FOR_IA_05072019.mrc:66212332:1159","amazon:0465005594","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part24.utf8:114291329:800","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-004.mrc:272086332:2556","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_045.bib.mrc:86729072:3471"],"title":"The physics of Star Trek","lccn":["95033266"],"notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Includes bibliographical references (p. 175-176) and index."},"number_of_pages":188,"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"url":["http://www.h-net.org/review/hrev-a0a1r4-aa"],"isbn_10":["0465005594"],"publish_date":"1995","publish_country":"nyu","by_statement":"Lawrence M. Krauss ; with a foreword by Stephen Hawking.","works":[{"key":"/works/OL50656W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"uris":["http://www.h-net.org/review/hrev-a0a1r4-aa"],"oclc_numbers":["32925425"],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"If you enjoy watching Star Trek, you're in good company. Some of the most distinguished physicists in the world, from Kip Thorne to Steven Weinberg and Sheldon Glashow, tune in, and a popular pastime at professional physics meetings and over e-mail is a discussion of the science in the series. Now you can join the fun.\n\nAnyone who has ever wondered, \"could this really happen?\" will gain useful insights into the Star Trek universe (and, incidentally, the real world of physics) in this charming and accessible guide. Lawrence M. Krauss boldly goes where Star Trek has gone - and beyond. He uses the Star Trek future as a launching pad to discuss the forefront of modern physics today.\n\nFrom Newton to Hawking, from Einstein to Feynman, from Kirk to Picard, Krauss leads you on a voyage to the world of physics as we now know it and as it might one day be.\n\nWith a foreword by the most renowned Trekker of all (and one-time Next Generation bit player), Stephen Hawking, and featuring a section on the top ten physics bloopers and blunders in Star Trek as selected by Nobel Prize-winning physicists and other dedicated Trekkers, this is a volume that will add a whole new dimension to your enjoyment of the series and to your appreciation of the universe we live in!"},"latest_revision":14,"revision":14,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-04-01T03:28:50.625462"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2026-07-26T11:49:14.975378"}}},"OLID:OL7593006M":{"bib_key":"OLID:OL7593006M","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL7593006M/The_Physics_of_Star_Trek","preview":"restricted","preview_url":"https://archive.org/details/physicsofstartre00lawr","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.or